00:01:14.380 --> 00:01:16.840
Most campuses are cutting or thinking about cuts

00:01:16.840 --> 00:01:18.920
and hoping someone in politics notices

00:01:18.920 --> 00:01:21.440
before the provincial vote on October 24th.

00:01:21.860 --> 00:01:25.080
In Hillside, Illinois, the Chicago Archdiocese this year

00:01:25.080 --> 00:01:27.140
dedicated a memorial called at peace

00:01:27.140 --> 00:01:30.200
at Queen of Heaven Catholic Cemetery, the Guardian reports.

00:01:30.900 --> 00:01:35.040
The Archdiocese says no other American Archdiocese has put up a memorial like it.

00:01:35.420 --> 00:01:38.660
It honors the Reverend Charles Ruby and people who died by suicide.

00:01:39.300 --> 00:01:44.740
Ruby started loving outreach to survivors of suicide in 1979, run by Catholic charities

00:01:44.740 --> 00:01:45.960
of the Archdiocese.

00:01:46.160 --> 00:01:50.460
He says the church has turned around completely and that those families are no longer condemned

00:01:50.460 --> 00:01:51.620
or thought to be in hell.

00:01:52.120 --> 00:01:54.700
Catholic teaching still treats killing oneself as a sin.

00:01:54.700 --> 00:01:59.860
It also says mental health problems can lead someone there, and that salvation remains possible.

00:02:00.360 --> 00:02:07.780
A 1917 church law, revised in 1983, had kept people who died by suicide from Catholic burial on consecrated ground.

00:02:08.280 --> 00:02:14.680
Chicago-born Pope Elio XIV has set some of his monthly prayer intentions on mental health and suicidal thoughts,

00:02:14.960 --> 00:02:17.180
and he has asked the church to listen without judging.

00:02:17.180 --> 00:02:22.300
The Reverend James Martin, editor at large of the Jesuit magazine America, says the

00:02:22.300 --> 00:02:26.700
church's stance now looks like the wider society's sympathy for everyone touched by a suicide.

